    description: "A grassland is a biome dominated primarily by grasses and other low-growing herbaceous plants, with comparatively few scattered trees, generally occurring in regions that receive moderate annual rainfall, enough to reliably support healthy grass growth but typically not quite sufficient to sustain substantial widespread forest cover. Grasslands occur across many distinct regions throughout the world under several different regional names, including the prairies of North America, the steppes of Central Asia, the pampas of South America, and the African savanna, with each particular regional grassland type supporting its own genuinely distinctive combination of locally adapted plant and resident animal species. Many grassland ecosystems depend heavily on periodic natural disturbance, particularly recurring fire and sustained grazing pressure from resident large herbivores, both of which help actively prevent the gradual encroachment of trees and woody shrubs that might otherwise eventually transform the open grassland into a considerably denser forest over time. Grassland soils are frequently exceptionally fertile, since the deep, extensive root systems characteristic of most native grasses continuously contribute substantial organic matter directly to the surrounding soil, which directly explains why many of the world's most agriculturally important grain-producing regions today were originally native grassland before conversion to large-scale farming. Grasslands worldwide have experienced dramatic and quite substantial habitat loss over the past two centuries, primarily due to extensive conversion into cropland and grazing pasture for livestock, making grassland restoration and ongoing conservation an increasingly important and active priority for many wildlife and ecosystem management organizations today."
